NAME
  sh - Freax command interpreter

SYNOPSIS
  sh
  sh FILE [ARG...]
  sh -c COMMAND [NAME [ARG...]]

DESCRIPTION
  sh runs interactive commands or shell scripts. Script mode does not load
  /etc/profile.lua or print the login banner.

  FILE sets $0 to its resolved path. ARG values become $1, $2, and so on.
  With -c, NAME becomes $0; it defaults to sh.

EXPANSION
  Environment variables use $NAME or ${NAME}. Single quotes suppress
  expansion. $? is the last exit status. $# is the argument count. $0 and
  $1 through $9 select positional parameters; use ${10} for larger indexes.
  $* joins all parameters. "$@" as a complete word preserves each parameter
  as one argument.

  '*' and '?' perform pathname expansion. '~' expands to $HOME. Backticks
  perform command substitution and remove trailing newlines.

COMMANDS
  Commands may be joined with ';', '&&', or '||'. '!' negates status.
  Pipelines use '|'. Redirects include '<', '>', '>>', '2>', and '2>&1'.

  Aliases apply at command positions. Builtins include cd, export, unset,
  alias, unalias, source, jobs, wait, kill, exit, and logout.

COMPOUND COMMANDS
  Compound keywords must appear at line boundaries.

    if COMMAND; then
      COMMANDS
    else
      COMMANDS
    fi

    for NAME in WORDS; do
      COMMANDS
    done

    for NAME; do
      COMMANDS
    done

  The last form iterates over positional parameters. Compound commands may
  nest up to 15 levels.

SOURCE
  source FILE [ARG...]
  . FILE [ARG...]

  Source runs FILE in the current shell, so aliases, environment changes,
  and working-directory changes remain. Supplied arguments temporarily
  replace positional parameters; without arguments, current parameters are
  inherited. Source nesting is limited to 10 levels.

LIMITATIONS
  Background '&', while/until, functions, case, traps, here-documents,
  arithmetic expansion, and general prefix/suffix use around "$@" are not
  implemented yet. Builtins cannot be pipeline stages.

EXAMPLES
  sh /tmp/build.sh input "two words"
  sh -c 'ls /bin && echo ready'
